        {
          "kind": "heading",
          "text": "Australia July 2026 All groups CPI annual movement"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The target is the ABS first-print All groups CPI annual movement for July 2026, rounded to one decimal. All anchors use the same national weighted-average-of-eight-capital-cities All groups annual CPI variant; the ledger sourceBinding points to the June 2026 Consumer Price Index page rather than the July monthly-indicator page, but the forecast remains tied to abs.cpi.all_groups.yoy.2026-07.first_print."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "ABS future-release calendar lookup for the target publication date and reference period.",
          "result": "The ABS calendar lists Consumer Price Index, Australia for reference period July 2026 at 11:30am AEST on Wednesday 26 August 2026."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "ABS May 2026 CPI release lookup for the recent All groups annual-movement history.",
          "result": "ABS reports All groups annual CPI of 3.8% in December 2025, 3.8% in January 2026, 3.7% in February, 4.6% in March, 4.2% in April, and 4.0% in May 2026."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "ABS May 2026 CPI release lookup for monthly movement and component evidence.",
          "result": "ABS reports May 2026 All groups CPI at -0.7% month on month in original terms and 4.0% year on year; Housing was 6.5%, Transport 3.3%, and trimmed mean inflation 3.6% year on year."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The reference class/base rate is the recent complete-monthly All groups annual-CPI sequence, centred near 3.9% over December-May. July 2025's unusually large 1.3% monthly increase is the dominant mechanical comparison effect, so a lower July 2026 annual rate is more likely even if underlying housing pressure persists."
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "text": "Prior/update/interval: Persistence prior is the December 2025-May 2026 All groups annual-rate mean of 4.02%; the historical sample is April 2025-May 2026 annual rates, whose successive changes have sample sigma = 0.46 percentage points. The base-effect adjustment for July 2025's +1.3% monthly print is -0.7pp after allowing a positive July 2026 monthly print, while persistent Housing (+6.5%) and trimmed mean (+3.6%) add +0.0pp net versus the recent level, implying 3.3%. The ordinary 80% half-width is 1.28*0.46 = 0.59pp; I use 0.8pp (1.36x) because a large July base month makes the annual-rate mapping unusually sensitive to the unobserved July monthly print, giving 2.5% to 4.1%."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Upside risk is a renewed energy or housing-price jump that produces a July monthly increase well above the assumed positive print; downside risk is another transport-led fall combined with weak discretionary prices. Either would land outside the interval: a July monthly rise near or above the July 2025 1.3% would land above 4.1%, while a material monthly fall would land below 2.5%."
        },
        {
          "kind": "forecast",
          "point": 3.3,
          "ciLow": 2.5,
          "ciHigh": 4.1
        }
