      "reasoning": [
        {
          "kind": "heading",
          "text": "Australia July 2026 headline CPI forecast"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The target is the first-print July 2026 ABS All groups CPI annual movement for Australia, rounded to one decimal. All anchors use the same headline, not-seasonally-adjusted All groups variant. The ledger calls the release the Monthly CPI Indicator and supplies a future indicator URL, while the official ABS calendar labels it Consumer Price Index, Australia; the sourceBinding also incorrectly points to the June release, so the forecast retains the registered target and records the discrepancy."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Fetch the ABS May 2026 Consumer Price Index, Australia release and its All groups history table.",
          "result": "ABS reported annual All groups CPI of 4.6% in March 2026, 4.2% in April 2026, and 4.0% in May 2026; monthly movements were 1.1%, 0.4%, and -0.7%, respectively."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Fetch the ABS monthly and annual All groups CPI history displayed with the May 2026 release.",
          "result": "The official history gives annual inflation of 2.4% in April 2025, 2.1% in May, 1.9% in June, 3.0% in July, 3.2% in August, 3.6% in September, 3.8% in October, 3.4% in November, 3.8% in December, 3.8% in January 2026, 3.7% in February, 4.6% in March, 4.2% in April, and 4.0% in May."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Inspect the ABS group contributions and recent headline components in the May 2026 release.",
          "result": "In May 2026, Housing inflation was 6.5%, Food and non-alcoholic beverages 3.3%, and Transport 3.3%; Transport had been 8.9% in March and 6.6% in April."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Verify the July 2026 reference-period release date in the ABS future releases calendar.",
          "result": "The ABS calendar schedules Consumer Price Index, Australia, reference period July 2026, for Wednesday 26 August 2026 at 11:30am AEST."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The reference class is the 14 official annual prints from April 2025 through May 2026. Its simple persistence base rate is the latest 4.0% print, but July 2025's unusually large 1.3% monthly increase will leave the twelve-month comparison. Assuming roughly 0.3% monthly inflation in both June and July 2026, June 2025's 0.1% base adds about 0.2 point before July 2025's 1.3% base subtracts about 1.0 point, implying approximately 3.2%."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Level and momentum remain firm because May annual CPI was 4.0% and Housing was 6.5%. The principal one-off is the July 2025 base effect; easing Transport inflation reinforces the decline. Policy works only with a lag over this horizon, so no separate large policy adjustment is imposed."
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence model prior = 4.0%, using the 14-print April 2025\u2013May 2026 official history. Adjustment components are +0.2 percentage point for replacing June 2025's 0.1% monthly rise with an assumed 0.3%, then -1.0 point for replacing July 2025's 1.3% rise with an assumed 0.3%: 4.0 + 0.2 - 1.0 = 3.2%. Successive annual-rate changes are -0.3, -0.2, +1.1, +0.2, +0.4, +0.2, -0.4, +0.4, 0.0, -0.1, +0.9, -0.4, and -0.2 percentage point; their sample standard deviation is sigma = 0.476. The empirical 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 0.610, giving 3.2 \u00b1 0.61 = [2.59, 3.81], rounded to [2.6%, 3.8%]."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Upside risk comes from another unusually large July price increase, persistent Housing inflation, or renewed fuel pressure and would land above the interval. Downside risk comes from a flat or negative July monthly print combined with broader goods disinflation and could land below the interval. An annual print outside the interval would require the two-month June\u2013July price change to differ materially from the roughly 0.6% central assumption."
        },
        {
          "kind": "forecast",
          "point": 3.2,
          "ciLow": 2.6,
          "ciHigh": 3.8
        }
      ]
