      "reasoning": [
        {
          "kind": "heading",
          "text": "July 2026 real average hourly earnings forecast"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The resolver is BLS Real Earnings Table A-1, not the production-and-nonsupervisory Table A-2: the required variant is all employees on private nonfarm payrolls, seasonally adjusted, over-the-month percent change in real average hourly earnings. Table A-1 uses CPI-U as its deflator."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Fetched the BLS 2026 release calendar to verify the publication date for the target release.",
          "result": "The BLS calendar lists Real Earnings for July 2026 at 08:30 AM on Wednesday, August 12, 2026; it also lists the July Employment Situation on August 7, 2026."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Fetched current BLS Real Earnings Table A-1 for the latest published all-employees, seasonally adjusted observations.",
          "result": "Table A-1 reports real average hourly earnings over-the-month changes of -0.6 percent in March 2026, -0.5 percent in April, and -0.1 percent in May; May nominal average hourly earnings rose 0.3 percent while CPI-U rose 0.5 percent."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Fetched the BLS June 2026 Employment Situation as the latest official nominal-wage signal before the July reference month.",
          "result": "BLS reported that average hourly earnings for all private nonfarm employees rose 13 cents, or 0.3 percent, in June 2026, to $37.64; year-over-year growth was 3.5 percent."
        },
        {
          "kind": "tool",
          "tool": "official.lookup",
          "call": "Fetched the BLS May 2026 Real Earnings release chart and table for the same Table A-1 reference class.",
          "result": "The official chart reports available May 2025-May 2026 monthly real-average-hourly-earnings changes of 0.4, -0.1, 0.1, 0.1, -0.1, -0.3, 0.3, 0.0, -0.6, -0.5, and -0.1 percent; the May release states the latest month was -0.1 percent."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Base rate/reference class: the available eleven monthly Table A-1 changes average about -0.07 percent and are centered near zero, with recent March-through-May inflation outpacing nominal hourly-pay growth. The June 0.3 percent nominal wage increase offsets part of that recent weakness but does not by itself imply a positive real print."
        },
        {
          "kind": "math",
          "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is the available May 2025-May 2026 Table A-1 monthly-change sample [0.4,-0.1,0.1,0.1,-0.1,-0.3,0.3,0.0,-0.6,-0.5,-0.1], with mean -0.07 percent; adjustment components are June's 0.3 percent nominal hourly-pay momentum, likely near-matching CPI-U inflation, and composition/seasonal noise. For this change series, sigma = 0.31 percentage points (sample standard deviation of the fetched values); 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.31 = 0.40 percentage points. Rounding the implied -0.07 percent center to the release's one-decimal convention gives -0.1 percent and bounds of -0.5 to 0.3 percent."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Upside risk: a July nominal-pay gain around 0.4 percent with CPI-U inflation near 0.1 percent would land at about 0.3 percent. Downside risk: a renewed CPI-U increase near 0.7 percent alongside only 0.1 percent nominal wage growth would land below the interval, around -0.6 percent. A large industry-composition shift could also put the first print outside the interval."
        },
        {
          "kind": "forecast",
          "point": -0.1,
          "ciLow": -0.5,
          "ciHigh": 0.3
        }
      ]
